\section{Conclusions}
\label{sec:conclusion}
We have presented the first dedicated cross-matching analysis between two galaxy merger catalogs and the Fermi-LAT 4FGL-DR4 gamma-ray source catalog~\cite{DR4}. The first catalog consists of a  sample of 70 galaxy pairs from the Canadian Network for Observational Cosmology Field Galaxy Redshift Survey and found no statistically significant associations within the 4$\sigma$ positional uncertainty threshold ($p < 0.05$).
The second catalog comprises of a high-confidence sample of 3,166 morphologically selected galaxy mergers~\cite{Ackermann2018} and the Fermi-LAT 4FGL-DR4 gamma-ray source catalog~\cite{DR4}. Using a conservative 4$\sigma$ positional uncertainty criterion and a Poisson-based statistical framework, we identified 38 spatial coincidences, of which 21 exhibit \emph{p}-values below 0.05. These matches predominantly include well-known gamma-ray source classes, FSRQs, BL Lacs, and radio galaxies, supporting the hypothesis that merger-driven processes can fuel AGN activity and high-energy emission. Notably, five secure associations are with unclassified (unassociated) Fermi sources from the fourth FGL-DR4 catalog, suggesting that merging systems may host previously unrecognized gamma-ray populations.

While blazar-like AGN dominate the matched sample, the presence of radio galaxies, UIDs and the broad range of observed gamma-ray flux densities point to a diversity of emission mechanisms. These may include merger-induced starbursts, large-scale shocks, or obscured AGN activity, highlighting the need for deeper multi-wavelength follow-up~\cite{Torres2004,Ackermann2012}. 
The consistent appearance of unassociated sources within the secure sample underscores the value of galaxy merger catalogs as informative priors for gamma-ray source identification. As gamma-ray localization continues to improve and future optical surveys such as Vera Rubin LSST, Euclid,  and DESI expand the census of galaxy mergers, the ability to resolve such associations will significantly increase. We shall also conduct a targeted search for some specific merger candidates, such as UGC 12914/5, UGC 813/6, and VV 114 in a forthcoming work.

