\section{SDSS Flux Conversion Methodology}
\label{appendix:sdss_flux_conversion}

SDSS magnitudes are reported in the {\tt asinh} magnitude system~\cite{Lupton1999}, which handle low S/N and negative fluxes more robustly than traditional Pogson magnitudes. The inverse asinh formula for converting $r$-band apparent magnitudes ($m_r$) to fluxes is:
\begin{equation}
    \frac{f_r}{f_0} = 2b \, \sinh\left(\frac{-2.5 \, m_r}{\ln(10)} - \ln(b)\right),
\end{equation}
where $f_0$ is the reference flux (1 maggy by definition), and $b = 1.2 \times 10^{-10}$ is the softening parameter for the $r$-band (see Table 21 in~\cite{Stoughton2002}). 

For completeness, the forward {\tt asinh} magnitude definition is:
\begin{equation}
    m = -\frac{2.5}{\ln(10)} \left[ \text{asinh}\left(\frac{f/f_0}{2b}\right) + \ln(b) \right].
\end{equation}

The resulting fluxes, in units of nanomaggies, were converted to Janskys using the SDSS relation:
\[
1\,\mathrm{nMgy} = 3.631 \times 10^{-6}\,\mathrm{Jy},
\]
and subsequently to cgs units via:
\[
1\,\mathrm{Jy} = 10^{-23}\,\mathrm{erg\,s^{-1}\,cm^{-2}\,Hz^{-1}}.
\]


