Importantly, although the value of the potential is constrained, the bound does not imply anything on the derivatives.
A priori, flat regions -- including mestastable de Sitter (dS) minima and maxima -- are allowed, as long as such flat regions are well within the interval in eq.~(\ref{eq:interval});
see fig.~\ref{fig.: metastable dS - limiting case}.
A key point in our analysis is that we expect all constants in eqs.~(\ref{varphi - upper bound}, \ref{varphi - lower bound}) to be order-one: the species-scale falloff rate is conjectured to be bounded as $\lambda \leq 1/\sqrt{d-2}$ \cite{vandeHeisteeg:2023ubh, Calderon-Infante:2023ler}, and we know that in the limiting case $p = q = 1/(d-1)$.
This expectation is also supported by comparison with other theorized general properties of UV-consistent EFTs \cite{Ooguri:2018wrx, Bedroya:2019snp, Rudelius:2021azq, vandeHeisteeg:2023uxj}, as we discuss below.


