Next, we examine an application of the SPARSE algorithm to a relatively simple system with two coupled channels.
The dimensions are $[\text{Energy}]$ and $[\text{Length}]=[\text{Energy}]^{-1}$ with arbitrary units.
The two channels are described in Table~\ref{tab:channels}. The potential matrix is
\begin{equation}
V(r) =
\begin{pmatrix}
V_{11}(r)	& V_{12}(r)	\\
V_{21}(r)	& V_{22}(r)	\\
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}
$V_{11}(r)$ is a potential well with a short-distance repulsion,
\begin{equation}
V_{11}(r) = \biggl(\frac{0.05}{r} - 100\biggr) \exp\bigl(-(100 r)^2\bigr)
\end{equation}
$V_{22}(r)$ is the same potential as $V_{11}(r)$ but shifted by $+100$,
\begin{equation}
V_{22}(r) = V_{11}(r) + 100
\end{equation}
