\begin{rem} In this final part, it is entirely possible to express everything in terms of direct images of currents, starting from 
$$\tilde{f}_{*}(\alpha^{*}(\phi'^{*}(\xi)))=\bar{f}_{*}(\alpha_{*}\alpha^{*}\phi'^{*}((\xi)))$$
The projection formula
$$\alpha_{*}(\alpha^{*}(\phi'^{*}(\omega^{m}_{\overline Z}))\otimes{\mathcal O}_{Z_2})=\phi'^{*}(\omega^{m}_{\overline Z})\otimes {\mathcal L}^{0}_{\overline Z}$$
shows that the form $\alpha_{*}\alpha^{*}\phi'^{*}((\xi))$ defines a section of the sheaf $\phi'^{*}(\omega^{m}_{Z})\otimes {\mathcal L}^{0}_{\overline Z}$. Hence,
$$\hat\phi_{*}\tilde{f}_{*}(\alpha_{*}(\alpha^{*}(\phi'^{*}(\omega^{m}_{\overline Z}))\otimes{\mathcal O}_{Z_2}))=\hat\phi_{*}\bar{f}_{*}(\phi'^{*}(\omega^{m}_{Z})\otimes {\mathcal L}^{0}_{\overline Z}))=f_{*}(\phi'_{*}(\phi'^{*}(\omega^{m}_{Z})\otimes {\mathcal L}^{0}_{\overline Z}))=f_{*}(\omega^{m}_{Z_1})$$
since $\phi'_{*}{\mathcal L}^{0}_{\overline Z}={\mathcal O}_{Z_{1}}$ by the normality of $Z_1$. We therefore deduce, $S$ being of type ${\mathfrak {F.R}}^{*}$, that $\hat\phi_{*}{\rm Tr}_{\tilde f}(\psi^{*}(\xi))$ is a section of the sheaf ${\mathcal L}^{m}_{S\times U}$, or equivalently, that ${\rm Tr}_{\tilde f}(\psi^{*}(\xi))$ is a section of the sheaf ${\mathcal L}^{m}_{\widetilde {S}\times U}=\omega^{m}_{\widetilde {S}\times U}$. 
We conclude that $\psi^{*}(\xi)$ defines a section of $\omega^{m}_{Z_2}$. But since $Z_2$ is of type ${\mathfrak {F.R}}^{*}$, $\psi^{*}(\xi)$ is a section of the sheaf ${\mathcal L}^{m}_{Z_2}$ and, consequently, $\xi$ is a section of ${\mathcal L}^{m}_{Z_1}$. Thus, $Z_1$ is of type ${\mathfrak {F.R}}^{*}$, and hence $Z$ is of type ${\mathfrak {F.R}}$.\vspace{2mm}

\noindent

Since the bases are normal, the compositions $\pi\circ \phi_{1}$, ${\tilde\pi}\circ \phi_{2}$, and ${\tilde\pi}\circ \phi'_{1}$ are still geometrically flat, being equidimensional over normal bases. In particular, they are all open. Moreover, the modifications $\Theta'$ and $\Theta"$ have connected fibers since $Z_1$ is normal.\vspace{1mm}  

\noindent If ${\overline{Z}}$ is normal, it is of type ${\mathfrak {F.R}}^{*}$ because $\tilde{Z}$ is of type ${\mathfrak {F.R}}$ and $\phi'_{1}$ is a finite modification. We then apply the previous results to deduce that $Z_1$ is of type ${\mathfrak {F.R}}^{*}$.  
But since a reduced complex space is of type ${\mathfrak {F.R}}$ if and only if its normalization is of type ${\mathfrak {F.R}}^{*}$ according to \corollaryref{C5}, it follows that $Z$ is of type ${\mathfrak {F.R}}$.\vspace{1mm}

\noindent 
Otherwise, ${\overline{Z}}$ is only of type ${\mathfrak {F.R}}$ because $\tilde{Z}$ is of type ${\mathfrak {F.R}}$ and $\phi'_{1}$ is a finite modification. But $Z_{2}$ being of type ${\mathfrak {F.R}}^{*}$, which by {\bf(i)} implies that $Z_{1}$ is also of type ${\mathfrak {F.R}}^{*}$ and, therefore, $Z$ is of type ${\mathfrak{F.R}}$. To see this, consider the diagram 
$$\xymatrix{Z_{2}\ar[d]_{\pi_{1}}\ar[r]^{\Theta"}&Z_{1}\ar[d]_{\pi_{2}}\\
\tilde{S}\ar[r]_{\nu}&S}$$
and localize it as in {\bf(i)} 
$$\xymatrix{{Z_2}\ar[r]^{\Theta}\ar[d]_{\tilde f}&{Z_1}\ar[d]^{f}\\
{\tilde  S}\times U\ar[r]_{\tilde{\nu}:=\nu\otimes {Id}}&S\times U}$$ 
to observe that the sections ${\rm Tr}_{\tilde f}(\Theta"^{*}(\xi))$ and $\nu^{*}({\rm Tr}_{f}(\xi))$ of ${\mathcal L}^{m}_{\tilde{S}\times U}$ coincide generically and thus globally due to the absence of torsion. The same reasoning applies to show that $\Theta"^{*}(\xi)$ defines a section of the sheaf $\omega^{m}_{Z_2}\,\blacksquare$
\end{rem}
