\section{Numerical Simulation}

This experiment is designed to support Corollary \ref{cor1}, which states that any near-maximizer of TAP free energy must lie close to the ridge regression solution. 

We considered the model shown in the beginning of section II and fixed the aspect ratio $\alpha = n/p = 2$ and the noise variance $\Delta = 0.1,0.5$. For each $p \in \{10, 20, \dots, 400\}$, we generated a single instance of $\mathbf{x}$, $\beta$, and $y$, computed the ridge solution and performed optimization of the TAP free energy from random initializations. We repeated this procedure 10 times and reported the average normalized distance between the TAP maximizer and the ridge estimator.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.45\textwidth]{1.png}
\includegraphics[width=0.45\textwidth]{2.png}
\caption{Numerical results under $\Delta = 0.1,0.5$, $\alpha = 2$.}
\label{fig:two_experiments}
\end{figure}

As shown in Figure \ref{fig:two_experiments}, the normalized distance between the TAP and ridge solutions decreases as $p$ increases, providing empirical support for Corollary \ref{cor1}.

\section{Conclusion}
Our results establish that under suitable conditions, the TAP free energy admits a maximizer whose value converges to the true free energy. This work advances the theoretical understanding of the TAP free energy approximation in high-dimensional Bayesian inference. By establishing its equivalence to the replica symmetric formula, this study enhances the applicability of the TAP framework for high-dimensional Bayesian inference, offering a more accurate alternative to the widely used mean-field approximation.

Although we have proven that the TAP free energy converges in probability to the true free energy, the behavior of its maximizer under the uniform spherical prior remains to be explored.
%less understood. 
In particular, while the TAP maximizer coincides with the posterior mean in the case of a Gaussian prior,
Corollary~\ref{cor1} suggests that the same holds under the spherical prior, though a rigorous justification requires additional work.
% In particular, unlike the case with a Gaussian prior, where the TAP maximizer coincides with the posterior mean, it is not known 
% remains to explore
% whether the TAP maximizer under the spherical prior converges to the corresponding posterior mean. 


Future research should aim to extend the TAP framework to encompass general prior distributions, including non-spherical and non-Gaussian priors, and to investigate its applicability in more complex settings. An immediate direction of interest is the extension of the linear TAP framework to general i.i.d. priors, as conjectured in \cite{Krzakala2014}.  Another important future direction is to develop and analyze algorithms for optimizing the TAP free energy in this non-convex regime. Understanding the geometry of the TAP landscape and designing robust optimization methods that can navigate potential spurious local maxima are essential steps toward practical inference in high-dimensional settings.



