\begin{abstract}


The neutron-proton effective mass splitting ($\Delta m^*_{np}$) is investigated through analyses of heavy-ion collisions using the improved quantum molecular dynamics (ImQMD) model with both standard and extended Skyrme interactions. By uncovering the strong correlation between the slope of the neutron-to-proton yield ratio with respect to the kinetic energy (i.e., $S_{n/p} $) and $\Delta m^*_{np}$, %with correlation coefficients exceeding 0.80. For the $^{124}\mathrm{Sn}+^{124}\mathrm{Sn}$ system, this correlation reaches 0.928. By comparing theoretical predictions with experimental data, 
we reveal that the constraints of the neutron-proton effective mass splitting via heavy ion collisions depend on the kinetic energy region of the emitted nucleons. At low kinetic energies, the data favor $m_n^*>m_p^*$ which is consistent with the nucleon-nucleus scattering analysis, while at high kinetic energies, they favor $m_n^*<m_p^*$. Our findings partly resolve the longstanding discrepancy in the constraints of neutron-proton effective mass splitting with heavy ion collisions and nucleon-nucleus scattering, and significantly advance the understanding of nucleon effective mass splitting through heavy ion collisions. %This finding provides the first direct evidence that the momentum-dependent symmetry potential likely decreases initially and then increases with momentum.



% The constraints of neutron-proton effective mass splitting ($\Delta m_{np}^*$) via heavy ion collisions are analyzed by using the version of the improved quantum molecular dynamics model (ImQMD), in which the standard and extended Skyrme interaction are used. Our calculations show that the slope of the emitted neutron to proton yield ratios with respect to the kinetic energy, $S_{n/p}$, is strongly correlated to the $\Delta m_{np}^*$, and the values of the correlation coefficients are greater than 0.80. For $^{124}\mathrm{Sn}+^{124}\mathrm{Sn}$, the value of the correlation coefficient between $S_{n/p}$ and $\Delta m_{np}^*$ can reach 0.928. By comparing the calculated $S_{n/p}$ to the data, we find that the data favor the parameter sets with different signs of neutron-proton effective mass splitting at different kinetic energy regions. At low kinetic energy region, the data favor the neutron effective mass greater than the proton effective mass, i.e., $m_n^*>m_p^*$, while the data at high kinetic energy favor the data sets with the neutron effective mass less than the proton effective mass, i.e., $m_n^*<m_p^*$. This finding implies the momentum-dependent symmetry potential may exhibit an initial decrease followed by an increase with momentum for well describing the slope of the emitted neutron to proton yield ratios with respect to the kinetic energy. %, or the effects of the high-momentum tail in the initial nucleus should be included in the future, .
\end{abstract}

