Up to now, the experimental constraints on the neutron-proton effective mass splitting are mainly determined via the following ways, such as the nucleon-nucleus scattering\cite{ChangXu2010PRC,XiaoHuaLi}, isoscalar and isovector giant resonance\cite{ZhangZhen2016PRC,KongHY2017,XuJunPRC2020,XuJun2020PLB}, and heavy ion collisions\cite{Morfouace2019,CYTSang2024,XieWJPRC2013,SuJun2016,YJP2024PRC}. In Figure ~\ref{Effe-methods}, we present part of the results from these experimental efforts. The analyses of nucleon-nucleus scattering data with optical potential models have limited the effective mass splitting at normal density as $\Delta m_{np}^*=\frac{m_n^*-m_p^*}{m}=(0.41\pm0.15)\delta$\cite{XiaoHuaLi}, here $\delta=(\rho_n-\rho_p)/(\rho_n+\rho_p)$ is the isospin asymmetry. For the above analysis, one should note that the results are for normal density and the nucleon kinetic energy in nuclear matter is less than 100 MeV\cite{BALi2018PPNP}. The Bayesian model analysis of isoscalar and isovector giant resonance information in $^{208} \mathrm{~Pb}$ has yielded an effective mass splitting $\Delta m_{n p}^*=$ $\left(0.084_{-0.123}^{+0.143}\right) \delta$\cite{XuJun2020PLB}. For HIC data, the Bayesian analysis of neutron and proton single ratios $R_{n / p}$ and double ratios $D R_{n / p}$, show that the parameter sets with the nucleon effective mass splitting $\Delta m_{n p}^*=(-0.05 \pm 0.09) \delta$\cite{Morfouace2019} can reproduce the data. More recently, a combination analysis by using the S$\pi$RIT experiment measurements of flow and stopping power data along with the ImQMD model was performed and constrained values of the effective mass splitting are $\Delta m_{n p}^*=\left(-0.07_{-0.06}^{+0.07}\right) \delta$\cite{CYTSang2024}, which is consistent with the constraints by using the single neutron to proton yield ratios ($R_{n/p}$) and double neutron to proton yield ratios ($DR(n/p)$) data. Obviously, the constraints on the sign and magnitude of effective mass splitting depend on the methods. On the side of HICs, the possible reasons for this discrepancy could be due to the complex collision mechanism, and the lack of sensitive experimental observables that is strongly correlated with the neutron-proton effective mass splitting. %Very recently, Junping Yang and others, by analyzing the slope of the energy spectra of emitted neutron-proton ratios at different energy regions, found that the information on effective mass splitting reflected by particles emitted in different energy regions supports different characteristics of effective interaction parameters: low energy data supports interaction parameters where $m_n^*>m_p^*$ while high energy data supports parameters where $m_n^*<m_p^*$\cite{YJP2024PRC}. These differences might be due to the current traditional observational measures and analysis methods not having high discernibility for nucleon effective mass, raising the question of whether more reliable and sensitive observational measures and analysis methods for heavy ion collisions can clarify this issue.

%\textcolor{blue}{It can be seen that there are still differences in the signs of effective mass splitting obtained through different methods; microscopic models and nuclear reaction-induced scattering data also indicate $m_n^*>m_p^*$ while heavy ion collisions provide central values for effective mass splitting around zero, corresponding to $m_n^*<m_p^*$. Recently, Junping Yang and others, by analyzing the slope of the energy spectra of emitted neutron-proton ratios at different energy regions, found that the information on effective mass splitting reflected by particles emitted in different energy regions supports different characteristics of effective interaction parameters: low energy data supports interaction parameters where $m_n^*>m_p^*$ while high energy data supports parameters where $m_n^*<m_p^*$\cite{YJP2024PRC}. These differences might be due to the current traditional observational measures and analysis methods not having high discernibility for nucleon effective mass, raising the question of whether more reliable and sensitive observational measures and analysis methods for heavy ion collisions can clarify this issue.}


%\textcolor{blue}{In terms of microscopic model calculations, ab initio methods provide important predictions for neutron-proton effective mass splitting. For example, the relativistic Brueckner-Hartree-Fock (RBHF) theory using projection methods\cite{Dalen2010PRC}, RBHF theory in full Dirac space, Brueckner-Hartree-Fock (BHF) theory\cite{WangSB2023PRC}, and extended Brueckner-Hartree-Fock (EBHF) theory\cite{ZuoW2005PRC}, as well as the self-consistent Green function (SCGF) method using the CD-Bonn potential\cite{Mansour2010POTP}, etc. Overall, the predictions from ab initio methods result in a positive neutron-proton effective mass splitting, with $m_n^*>m_p^*$ and greater than 0.16$\delta$, where $\delta$ represents the isospin asymmetry. Additionally, covariant density functional theories including the relativistic mean field (RMF) and relativistic Hartree-Fock (RHF) theories yield an average and standard deviation for the effective mass splitting $\Delta m_{np}^*=(m_n^*-m_p^*)/m$ of $(-0.09 \pm 0.03) \delta$ and $(-0.03 \pm 0.01) \delta$\cite{Margueron2018}, respectively, indicating a negative effective mass splitting where $m_n^*<m_p^*$. }

%\textcolor{blue}{On the other hand, various nuclear reactions and nuclear structure experiments are used to constrain the neutron-proton effective mass splitting. For example, nucleon-nucleon scattering \cite{XiaoHuaLi}and isovector giant dipole resonances\cite{ZhangZhen2016PRC}. Recent optical model analyses of nucleon-nucleus scattering data have limited the effective mass splitting to $(0.41\pm0.15)\delta$\cite{XiaoHuaLi}. Bayesian model analysis of isoscalar and isovector giant resonance information in ${ }^{208} \mathrm{~Pb}$ has yielded an effective mass splitting at a $68 \%$ confidence level of $\Delta m_{n p}^*=$ $\left(0.083_{-0.080}^{+0.081}\right) \delta$\cite{XuJun2020PRC}.}
