\subsection{Beyond conventional ETH and the emergence of unitary symmetry in truncated operators}\label{sec-eth-new}
Recent studies extend the conventional ETH which neglects correlations between the matrix elements of the observables, and a generalized form of ETH is introduced \cite{FoiniPRE,Pappalardi:2023nsj}. Let us consider a truncated operator
${\cal O}_{\Delta E} = P_{\Delta E} {\cal O} P_{\Delta E}$, where
\begin{equation}\label{eq-PDE}
P_{\Delta E}=\sum_{|E_{\alpha}-E_{0}|\le\Delta E/2}|\alpha\rangle\langle\alpha|.
\end{equation}
The general ETH  \cite{FoiniPRE,Pappalardi:2023nsj} suggests that below a sufficiently small energy scale $\Delta E_U$,  ${\cal O}_{\Delta E}$ can be described by a sample from Unitary Invariance Ensemble (UIE), 
\begin{equation}\label{eq-uie}
    {\cal O}_{\Delta E_U}\equiv{\cal O}_{U}=U{\cal O}^{*}U^{\dagger},
\end{equation}
where $U$ is a Haar-random unitary (or orthogonal) operator and ${\cal O}^\ast$ is some fixed, operator specific matrix which can be chosen to be diagonal without loss of generality.
${\cal O}_{\Delta E}$ will
be referred to as exhibiting emergent unitary symmetry
if it is identified as a sample of UIE. 

To characterize unitary symmetry, we consider submatrices of ${\cal O}_{\Delta E_U}$ by projecting it onto an even smaller energy window ${\cal O}_{\delta E} = P_{\delta E} {\cal O}_{\Delta E_U} P_{\delta E}$. 
If ${\cal O}_{\Delta E_U}$ can be described by UIE (as given in Eq.~\eqref{eq-uie}), 
spectral properties of ${\cal O}_{\delta E}$ are uniquely fixed by the spectrum of ${\cal O}_{\Delta E_U}$ and the ratio $d_{\delta E}/d_{\Delta E_U}$, where $d_{\delta E}=\text{Tr}[P_{\delta E}]$ and $d_U \equiv d_{\Delta E_U}=\text{Tr}[P_{\Delta E_U}]$. For example, in case of sufficiently large $d_{\delta E}$ the $k$th free cumulants of ${\cal O}_{\delta E}$ is given by \cite{wang2023emergence}
\begin{equation}\label{eq-fca}
    \Delta_{k}(\delta E)=\left(\frac{d_{\delta E}}{d_{U}}\right)^{k}\Delta_k^U,\ \text{for\ } \delta E \le \Delta E_U ,
\end{equation}
where $\Delta^U_k$ denotes the $k$th free cumulant of ${\cal O}_{\Delta E_U}$. 
Here free cumulants 
of an operator ${\cal O}$ is defined by making use of its moments ${\cal M}_k \equiv \frac{1}{d} \text{Tr}[{\cal O}^k]$ \cite{PhysRevLett.129.170603}, through the iterative relation
\begin{equation}\label{eq-fc}
\Delta_{k} = {\cal M}_{k}-\sum_{j=1}^{k-1}\Delta_{j}\sum_{a_{1}+a_{2}+\cdots a_{j}=k-j}{\cal M}_{a_{1}}\cdots{\cal M}_{a_{j}}\ .
\end{equation}

If $\Delta E_U$ is sufficiently small, the density of states within the microcanonical window can be regarded as constant. This yields $d_{\delta E}/d_{U}=\delta E/\Delta E_{U}$ which turns (\ref{eq-fca}) into 
\begin{equation}\label{eq-Deltak-DE}
\Delta_{k}(\delta E)=\left(\frac{\delta E}{\Delta E_{U}}\right)^{k}\Delta_{k}^{U} \propto \delta E^k .
\end{equation}
This is the main criterion we employ as indicator for the emergence of unitary symmetry. In addition, we also consider the indictor introduced in Ref.~\cite{PhysRevLett.123.260601}. It is proved that if ${\cal O}_{\Delta U}$ exhibits unitary symmetry, the probability distribution of diagonal and off-diagonal elements have the following relation
\begin{equation}\label{eq-uim-id2}
    P(\frac{{\cal O}_{\alpha\alpha}-{\cal O}_{\beta\beta}}{2})=P({\cal O}_{\alpha\beta}) .
\end{equation}



To numerically investigate the emergence of unitary symmetry in the truncated operator ${\cal O}_{\Delta E}$,  we first study the indicator given in Eq.~\eqref{eq-Deltak-DE}, where we calculate the free cumulants of ${\cal O}_{\delta E}$.
Throughout the numerical simulations, we will fix the center of the microcanonical energy window (used in Eq.~\eqref{eq-PDE}) as $E_0 = 0.0$.
Using the standard sparse-matrix diagonalization method, we obtain $2000$ eigenstates ($1000$ eigenstates for $j\ge 4000$) and project the observables ${\cal A}$ and ${\cal B}$ onto this energy window. 
The free cumulants $\Delta_k$ are then calculated via Eq.~\eqref{eq-fc}.

The results for $\Delta_k$  as a function of energy window width $\delta E$ are shown in Figs.~\ref{Fig-FC}, ~\ref{Fig-FCB}, \ref{Fig-FC-Int} and ~\ref{Fig-FCB-Int}, where we study observables ${\cal A}$ and ${\cal B}$ in chaotic and integrable cases.
The free cumulants exhibit markedly different behavior in the chaotic (Figs.~\ref{Fig-FC} and ~\ref{Fig-FCB}) and near integrable cases (Figs.~\ref{Fig-FC-Int} and ~\ref{Fig-FCB-Int}): the power-law dependence
$\Delta_k \propto \delta E^k$, is present only in the chaotic case but not in the near integrable case.
 This suggests the existence of unitary symmetry in the truncated operator ${\cal O}_{\Delta E}$ in the chaotic case, while it is absent in the integrable case.


