\section{Appendix:  Proof of Lemma~\ref{proseq}} 


    For any vertex $v \in \overset{\circ}{V}$, we have $v\mathrel{R}v$, so $\mathrel{R}$ is reflexive. Moreover, for any $v, u \in \overset{\circ}{V}$, if $v\mathrel{R}u$, then either $u = v$, or $u$ and $v$ are linked by a reticulation arc, or $u$ and $v$ share a child. Therefore, we also have $u\mathrel{R}v$, and thus $\mathrel{R}$ is symmetric. Thus, it remains to establish transitivity of $\mathrel{R}$. 

    Suppose that for $v, u, t \in \overset{\circ}{V}$, we have $v\mathrel{R}u$ and $u\mathrel{R}t$. We claim that $v \mathrel{R}t$.  This holds trivially if $u=v$,  while if $u \neq v$, there are three cases to consider.


    {\bf Case (i):} Suppose that $u$ and $v$ are linked by a reticulation arc where $v$ is the reticulate vertex. Given that  $u\mathrel{R}t$,
   if $u=t$, then $v \mathrel{R}t$; whereas if $u$ and $t$ share a child, the child must be $v$.  Otherwise, the children of $u$ are both reticulate vertices so $N$ is not a tree-child network. In other words, $v$ and $t$ are linked by a reticulation arc, $v \mathrel{R}t$. Finally, if $t$ and $u$ are linked by a reticulation arc, then $v=t$. Therefore, we have $v\mathrel{R}t$ (otherwise, the conditions of a tree-child network are violated).


   {\bf Case (ii):} Suppose that $u$ and $v$ are linked by a reticulation arc, where $u$ is the reticulate vertex. Given that $u\mathrel{R}t$,
    if $u=t$, then $v \mathrel{R}t$; whereas if $t$ and $u$ are linked by a reticulation arc, then $u$ must be a child of $t$. Therefore, $v$ and $t$ share a child $u$, $v\mathrel{R}t$.
     Note that $u$ and $t$ cannot share a child (otherwise, the conditions of tree-child network are violated).

 {\bf Case (iii):} Suppose that $u$ and $v$ share a child. Given that $u\mathrel{R}t$, 
    if $u=t$, then $v \mathrel{R}t$; wherease
    if $u$ and $t$ share a child, then $v$ and $t$ share a child too, so  $v\mathrel{R}t$.
        Alternatively, if  $t$ and $u$ are linked by a reticulation arc, then $t$ must be the shared child of $u$ and $v$. Thus, $t$ and $v$ are linked by a reticulation arc, so $v\mathrel{R}t$.

In summary, for any $v, u, t \in \overset{\circ}{V}$, if $v\mathrel{R}u$ and $u\mathrel{R}t$, then $v \mathrel{R}t$, thus $\mathrel{R}$ is transitive, and so $\mathrel{R}$  is an equivalence relation on $\overset{\circ}{V}$.

