\textbf{{\textit{Analysis of stress accumulation/release}:}}\\\\
Figure \ref{figure4a} represents the changes of surface shear stress $\tau_{12}$ against time $t$ for different creep velocities $V$ taking a fixed $\alpha=0.5$. The stress starts to drop when observed at $t=115$ years after fault movement at $114.01$ years with different rates depending on the creep velocity. The shear stress $\tau_{12}$ when creep velocity $V$ is $0.04$ m/year (blue line) shows the fastest decrease in stress, while for $V=0.02$ m/year (black line), it shows comparatively slower decrease. Figure \ref{figure4b} illustrates the evolution of surface shear stress $\tau_{12}$ over time in a viscoelastic medium for different values of the fractional order $\alpha$, taking a fixed creep velocity $V=0.02$ m/year. A rapid stress relaxation has been shown for $\alpha=1.0$ while this relaxation is slowest for $\alpha=0.1$, i.e., the system exhibits a more substantial memory effect, leading to slower stress dissipation for decreasing value of $\alpha$. 
